{"kind":"request","text":"CTL-1: the control helper classifies a failed `.resolves` or\n`.rejects` as an assertion failure.\n\nThe fact at main bc79b6aa. `scripts/control.mjs` calls a failure an\nassertion when its message starts with `AssertionError`\n(line 167). Vitest makes an `AssertionError` for a promise that\nrejects under `.resolves` or resolves under `.rejects`, then gives\nit the stack of an earlier `Error`. The first line then starts\nwith `Error: promise rejected` or `Error: promise resolved`. The\nhelper answers INCONCLUSIVE for a control whose named tests failed\nby exactly that assertion. The C1 control of 14 November stopped\nthere (fe22243e), and each later control on a promise assertion\nstops the same way.\n\nWork, in one small head:\n\n1. The helper classifies the two Vitest messages as assertion\n   failures: \"promise rejected ... instead of resolving\" and\n   \"promise resolved ... instead of rejecting\". Use the error name\n   of the Vitest report when the report holds it. Otherwise match\n   the two exact message forms of the pinned `@vitest/expect`.\n2. A thrown error that is no assertion stays inconclusive, as\n   today.\n3. Tests in the helper's own test file: each of the two forms\n   gives DISTINGUISHES, and a plain thrown error gives\n   INCONCLUSIVE.\n4. `docs/testing.md` states the two forms in the row of the\n   verdict.\n\nOrder: after the heads of the client queue. The head can share\nthe gate of CUT-A2. It holds nothing.","body":{"conditions":"scripts/control.mjs gives DISTINGUISHES for a named test that fails by a rejected .resolves or a resolved .rejects, and INCONCLUSIVE for a plain thrown error. Its tests cover the three cases. docs/testing.md states the forms. Gate passes and the checker reviews the head.","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fe22243e8855e5c52c3150ff5a40b0916de49df9 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:003802bea882e6c5c61885187